  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nOn Wednesday, a cold front trailing a deepening surface cyclone near \nHudson Bay will reach the Mid-Mississippi River Valley. Model \nguidance still varies on how quickly this front advances southward, \nbut there is a growing consensus that it will reach along or just \nsouth of the I-70 corridor around midday. Showers and thunderstorms \nare anticipated to be ongoing mainly behind the front Wednesday \nmorning before a relative minimum in coverage around midday into \nafternoon during a lapse in stronger large-scale forcing/ascent. \nSouth of the front, 850-hPa temperatures will remain anomalously \nwarm, but the position of the front and extent of cloud cover \ndecrease confidence in high temperatures, which could reasonably \nrange from the low to upper 90s F, per the NBM interquartile range. \nConfidence is high that dewpoints will increase on Wednesday with \npooled moisture in the vicinity of the front, but it is still \nuncertain at this point if 105+ F heat index values materialize. \nAlthough still possibly needed, a Heat Advisory has not been issued \nfor this reason.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will increase in coverage again sometime \nWednesday evening, eventually becoming widespread, as large-scale \nascent increases with falling mid-level heights ahead of an \napproaching upper-level trough and arrival of an equatorward jet \nentrance region. With a capping inversion across the warm sector and \nstrongest forcing, wind shear, and instability behind the front; it \nis uncertain if any severe, surface-based thunderstorms with \ndamaging winds will develop. Instead, the most organized and \nstrongest thunderstorms are favored to exist behind the front which \nmay limit threats to gusty winds and small hail. Anomalous PW \nexceeding 2\", deep warm cloud depths, and deep-layer flow \nparalleling the slow-moving front also supports heavier rainfall \nwhere thunderstorms with efficient, high rainfall rates train. \nThrough Thursday, the NBM and global ensemble models indicate that \nmost locations see 0.25 to 1\" of rainfall, with a swath of higher \namounts of 1 to 3\" somewhere near the I-44 and I-70 corridors. Since \nantecedent soil conditions are very dry, 3-hour FFG is high and generally\n3 to 4\", supporting heavier rainfall being more beneficial than\nproducing flash flooding.\n\nShowers and a few thunderstorms could linger well into the day \nThursday until an upper-level trough axis arrives and shunts the \nfront and precipitation more quickly southward. The highest \nprobabilities of rainfall in ensemble model guidance are along and \nsouth of I-44/I-70 during afternoon, which is where high \ntemperatures may struggle to reach above 80 F with post-frontal CAA \nand clouds as well. To the north, decreasing clouds will promote \nslightly warmer, but still much cooler high temperatures in the low-\n80s F.\n\nAfter Thursday, global model guidance is coming into better \nagreement on the upper-level wave pattern becoming quasi-zonal \nthrough the weekend and early next week with a front arriving and \nwavering across the broader Mid-Mississippi River Valley. This \npattern results in an extended period of at least low PoPs that \nbegins Friday evening. Ensemble model membership with measurable \nrainfall is highest in the Friday night-into-Saturday night \ntimeframe, but these members also show most of this period into \nearly next week being dry vs. wet. With this front and waves of \nclouds/precipitation, temperatures are not completely certain, but \nthe NBM interquartile range has been over the last 24 to 48 hours \nclosing in on above-average high temperatures mainly in the low-80s \nto low-90s F.",
